First: What is an AMI?

AMI = A pre-configured template to launch EC2 instances.
It contains:
OS (Linux/Ubuntu/Windows)
Pre-installed software (e.g., Apache, Nginx, Java, Python, MySQL)
Configuration (patches, drivers, security updates)
So, instead of installing everything manually each time, companies use AMIs to launch servers quickly and consistently.
🏢 Real-time Company Usage of AMIs

1) Base OS Images
Example: A bank’s cloud team may say:
"All new Linux servers must come from our hardened Ubuntu AMI with security patches and monitoring agent pre-installed."
Why? → Compliance, consistency, and security.
Companies maintain Golden AMIs (official, company-approved images).

2) Application-specific AMIs
DevOps teams build AMIs that already include:
App server (Tomcat, Node.js, Nginx)
Code artifacts (latest build of the app)
Security configs (firewall, IAM agent, antivirus)
Example:
An E-commerce company builds a custom AMI with Apache, PHP, and Magento → every time they scale (Auto Scaling Group), new servers come pre-installed and ready.

3) Auto Scaling & Load Balancers
AMIs are used with Auto Scaling Groups (ASG).
Example:
Netflix has an AMI with their video-streaming app.
When demand spikes, ASG launches more EC2 instances from that AMI instantly.

4) Disaster Recovery (DR)
Companies keep AMIs of critical systems.
Example: If a prod web server crashes, they launch a new one from the saved AMI → restores in minutes.

5) Migration & Testing
Lift-and-shift migration: create an AMI of an on-prem server and run it on AWS.
QA teams: create AMIs with pre-installed test tools → spin up test environments quickly.

📌 Types of AMIs Companies Use
AWS-provided AMIs (Amazon Linux, Ubuntu, Windows)
Marketplace AMIs (vendor software like Splunk, Palo Alto, MySQL, RedHat)
Custom/Golden AMIs (created in-house by Cloud/DevOps teams for security and compliance).
